---
title: Streaming Structured Output UIs
id: structured-outputs-streaming
order: 3
description: "Build a UI that fills in field by field as the model streams structured JSON. chat({ outputSchema, stream: true }) on the server, useChat({ outputSchema }) on the client — progressive partial state plus a typed terminal object."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - structured outputs
  - streaming
  - useChat outputSchema
  - partial
  - final
  - DeepPartial
  - progressive ui
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have an existing chat-style endpoint and you want the structured response to populate a UI "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"while"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the model is generating — a form filling in field by field, a card whose ingredients list grows as JSON streams in, a typewriter preview of a JSON-typed report. Blocking on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" would leave the UI dark until the whole object is ready; this guide is the alternative."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By the end you'll have a server endpoint streaming structured JSON as Server-Sent Events, and a client that reads a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" (progressive object) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" (completed terminal object) from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a full Start app that shows a table from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial.rows"},{"type":"text","value":", open "},{"type":"link","href":"../tutorials/streaming-structured-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming Structured Table"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This is the streaming counterpart of "},{"type":"link","href":"./one-shot","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-Shot Extraction"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If you don't need progressive UI updates, the one-shot path is simpler. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns and keep history, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-endpoint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server endpoint"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/api/extract-person/route.ts (or your framework's equivalent)\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That's the entire server side. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<typeof PersonSchema>>"},{"type":"text","value":" — an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable"},{"type":"text","value":" of standard streaming events plus a terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event carrying the completed object. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" knows what to do with it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-with-usechat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the same schema to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":". The hook gives you a progressively-parsed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" and a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, partial, final } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <form\n      onSubmit={(e) => {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\");\n      }}\n    >\n      <button disabled={isLoading}>Extract</button>\n      {/* `partial` fills in field by field as JSON streams in. */}\n      <p>Name: {partial.name ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Age: {partial.age ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Email: {partial.email ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      {final && <pre>Completed: {JSON.stringify(final, null, 2)}</pre>}\n    </form>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What the hook does for you:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>"},{"type":"text","value":" — every property optional, every nested array element optional. Updated from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" deltas via the runtime's partial-JSON parser. The hook derives it from the latest assistant message's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" part (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for why that distinction matters), so it reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage()"},{"type":"text","value":" and the first chunk without any extra reset state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null"},{"type":"text","value":" — the completed terminal payload from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" until the run completes successfully."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is used purely for client-side TypeScript inference. The streaming path does not run Standard Schema validation; validate the completed object in the consumer when required."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same shape works for "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-streaming adapters too"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If an adapter (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama) returns a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event with no incremental deltas, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" populates when the event arrives. Same consumer code. Claude Code and Codex emit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" from the harness event. OpenCode, Grok Build, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acpCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":" parse the last assistant text at the end. In both cases "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays empty until "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" is set. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Agents"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional: omit it and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" returns its standard shape without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rendering-reasoning-and-tool-calls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering reasoning and tool calls"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" cover the structured payload. Reasoning tokens and tool calls land where they would in any other chat — on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[…].parts"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chunk type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where it lands on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[i].parts"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"REASONING_MESSAGE_CONTENT"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ThinkingPart"},{"type":"text","value":" on the assistant message"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_START"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_ARGS"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_END"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallPart"},{"type":"text","value":" on the assistant message"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolResultPart"},{"type":"text","value":" on the tool message"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" (with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" set)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart"},{"type":"text","value":" on the assistant message — the JSON deltas accumulate into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.raw"},{"type":"text","value":" and the progressive parse populates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.partial"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" (no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextPart"},{"type":"text","value":" on the assistant message"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So render reasoning, tool calls, and the typed object from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[].parts"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" are shortcuts for the latest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" part only."}]},{"type":"code","value":"return (\n  <>\n    {messages.map((message) => (\n      <div key={message.id}>\n        {message.parts.map((part, i) => {\n          if (part.type === \"thinking\") {\n            return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n            return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"text\") {\n            return <p key={i}>{part.content}</p>;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"structured-output\") {\n            return (\n              <StructuredView key={i} data={part.data ?? part.partial} />\n            );\n          }\n          return null;\n        })}\n      </div>\n    ))}\n  </>\n);","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" part fields:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":": the validated object after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":": a progressive parse of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":" while JSON streams"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":": the source JSON string"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streaming"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" equals "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" on the latest assistant turn. Walk "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parts"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need history, tool calls, or reasoning."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migration note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Earlier versions of TanStack AI routed structured JSON deltas through a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextPart"},{"type":"text","value":" and required you to filter that part out of your renderer. That hack is gone — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" on a structured-output run now routes into a dedicated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart"},{"type":"text","value":" (with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":", optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorMessage"},{"type":"text","value":"). If your render loop still has an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"if (part.type === \"text\") return null;"},{"type":"text","value":" line specifically for hiding structured JSON, you can remove it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Going lower-level?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" still exposes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" if you want to observe individual chunks alongside the managed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" state (e.g. to drive a custom progress UI). Internal partial/final tracking runs first, then your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" callback fires with the same chunk — the two paths compose."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" (React, Vue, Solid) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChat"},{"type":"text","value":" (Svelte) all accept the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" option and expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same semantics — only the reactivity primitive differs (React state, Vue "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shallowRef"},{"type":"text","value":", Solid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Accessor"},{"type":"text","value":", Svelte reactive getter). See "},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the local idioms."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-the-stream-contains","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What the stream contains"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputStream<T>"},{"type":"text","value":". The stream includes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CUSTOM"},{"type":"text","value":" event named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":". The completion event carries the parsed object. A parsing failure emits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of a successful "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  type: \"CUSTOM\",\n  name: \"structured-output.complete\",\n  value: {\n    object: T;          // completed, parsed, typed\n    raw: string;        // full accumulated JSON text\n    reasoning?: string; // present only for thinking/reasoning models\n  },\n  // ...standard event fields (timestamp, …)\n  // model lives in metadata.tanstack when present\n}","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.start"},{"type":"text","value":" event fires once at the beginning of the run carrying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ messageId }"},{"type":"text","value":". Its job is to tell the client \"the next batch of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" deltas belongs to the assistant message with this id — route them into a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of building a free-form "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextPart"},{"type":"text","value":".\" The runtime also attaches the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messageId"},{"type":"text","value":" to the terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":" so the client snaps the right assistant message's part on the way out — that extra field isn't on the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputCompleteEvent<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" shape (since consumer code typically doesn't need it; the start event already carries it), but you can read it off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":" at runtime if you need to."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"adapter-coverage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter coverage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming structured output works with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":", but only some support a true single-request streaming wire format:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-openai"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Responses API, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text.format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-openrouter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-grok"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-groq"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-bedrock"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native stream through Converse or an OpenAI-compatible API"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-byteplus"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream on supported models; unsupported models emit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-llmgateway"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-cloudflare"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response_format: json_schema"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-lovable"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native single-request stream (Responses or Chat Completions)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other adapters (anthropic, gemini, ollama, …)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fallback: runs non-streaming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuredOutput"},{"type":"text","value":" and emits the final object as one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fallback path keeps the consumer code identical across providers — you always read the final object off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" — but you won't see incremental deltas unless the adapter implements "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuredOutputStream"},{"type":"text","value":" natively."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"advanced-iterating-the-stream-directly","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced: iterating the stream directly"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you don't need the SSE-over-HTTP boundary — Node scripts, CLIs, server endpoints that respond with a final JSON object instead of a stream, or tests — consume "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" as a plain async iterable:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John Doe is 30, john@example.com\" }],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  stream: true,\n});\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === \"CUSTOM\" && chunk.name === \"structured-output.complete\") {\n    // Typed against PersonSchema. Validate here when required.\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.name);\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.age);\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputStream<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" the server endpoint above hands to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":". Pick this shape when you're a single process end-to-end; use the server-endpoint-plus-"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" shape when there's a network in the middle."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Combining with tools?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" are all set, the agent loop runs first and the structured stream emits its terminal event only after every tool completes. Tool-approval gates and client-tool invocations work the same as in a normal chat — see "},{"type":"link","href":"./with-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pause/resume pattern."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"server-endpoint","text":"Server endpoint","level":2},{"id":"client-with-usechat","text":"Client with useChat","level":2},{"id":"rendering-reasoning-and-tool-calls","text":"Rendering reasoning and tool calls","level":2},{"id":"what-the-stream-contains","text":"What the stream contains","level":2},{"id":"adapter-coverage","text":"Adapter coverage","level":2},{"id":"advanced-iterating-the-stream-directly","text":"Advanced: iterating the stream directly","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have an existing chat-style endpoint and you want the structured response to populate a UI _while_ the model is generating — a form filling in field by field, a card whose ingredients list grows as JSON streams in, a typewriter preview of a JSON-typed report. Blocking on `await chat({ outputSchema })` would leave the UI dark until the whole object is ready; this guide is the alternative.\n\nBy the end you'll have a server endpoint streaming structured JSON as Server-Sent Events, and a client that reads a typed `partial` (progressive object) and `final` (completed terminal object) from `useChat`.\n\nFor a full Start app that shows a table from `partial.rows`, open [Streaming Structured Table](../tutorials/streaming-structured-table).\n\n> **Note:** This is the streaming counterpart of [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot). If you don't need progressive UI updates, the one-shot path is simpler. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns and keep history, see [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn).\n\n## Server endpoint\n\n```typescript\n// app/api/extract-person/route.ts (or your framework's equivalent)\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nThat's the entire server side. `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` returns a `StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<typeof PersonSchema>>` — an `AsyncIterable` of standard streaming events plus a terminal `structured-output.complete` event carrying the completed object. `toServerSentEventsResponse` knows what to do with it.\n\n## Client with `useChat`\n\nPass the same schema to `useChat`. The hook gives you a progressively-parsed `partial` and a typed `final`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, partial, final } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <form\n      onSubmit={(e) => {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\");\n      }}\n    >\n      <button disabled={isLoading}>Extract</button>\n      {/* `partial` fills in field by field as JSON streams in. */}\n      <p>Name: {partial.name ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Age: {partial.age ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Email: {partial.email ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      {final && <pre>Completed: {JSON.stringify(final, null, 2)}</pre>}\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nWhat the hook does for you:\n\n- **`partial`** is `DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>` — every property optional, every nested array element optional. Updated from `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas via the runtime's partial-JSON parser. The hook derives it from the latest assistant message's `structured-output` part (see [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) for why that distinction matters), so it reads `{}` between `sendMessage()` and the first chunk without any extra reset state.\n- **`final`** is `z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null` — the completed terminal payload from the `structured-output.complete` event. `null` until the run completes successfully.\n- **`outputSchema`** is used purely for client-side TypeScript inference. The streaming path does not run Standard Schema validation; validate the completed object in the consumer when required.\n- The same shape works for **non-streaming adapters too**. If an adapter (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama) returns a single `structured-output.complete` event with no incremental deltas, `partial` stays `{}` and `final` populates when the event arrives. Same consumer code. Claude Code and Codex emit `structured-output.complete` from the harness event. OpenCode, Grok Build, and `acpCompatible` parse the last assistant text at the end. In both cases `partial` stays empty until `final` is set. See [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n`outputSchema` is optional: omit it and `useChat` returns its standard shape without `partial` / `final`.\n\n## Rendering reasoning and tool calls\n\n`partial` / `final` cover the structured payload. Reasoning tokens and tool calls land where they would in any other chat — on `messages[…].parts`:\n\n| Chunk type | Where it lands on `messages[i].parts` |\n|---|---|\n| `REASONING_MESSAGE_CONTENT` | `ThinkingPart` on the assistant message |\n| `TOOL_CALL_START` / `_ARGS` / `_END` | `ToolCallPart` on the assistant message |\n| `TOOL_CALL_RESULT` | `ToolResultPart` on the tool message |\n| `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` (with `outputSchema` set) | `StructuredOutputPart` on the assistant message — the JSON deltas accumulate into `part.raw` and the progressive parse populates `part.partial` |\n| `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` (no `outputSchema`) | `TextPart` on the assistant message |\n\nSo render reasoning, tool calls, and the typed object from `messages[].parts`. `final` and `partial` are shortcuts for the latest `structured-output` part only.\n\n```tsx ignore\nreturn (\n  <>\n    {messages.map((message) => (\n      <div key={message.id}>\n        {message.parts.map((part, i) => {\n          if (part.type === \"thinking\") {\n            return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n            return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"text\") {\n            return <p key={i}>{part.content}</p>;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"structured-output\") {\n            return (\n              <StructuredView key={i} data={part.data ?? part.partial} />\n            );\n          }\n          return null;\n        })}\n      </div>\n    ))}\n  </>\n);\n```\n\nThe `structured-output` part fields:\n\n- `data`: the validated object after `structured-output.complete`\n- `partial`: a progressive parse of `raw` while JSON streams\n- `raw`: the source JSON string\n- `status`: `streaming`, `complete`, or `error`\n\n`final` equals `data` on the latest assistant turn. Walk `parts` when you need history, tool calls, or reasoning.\n\n> **Migration note:** Earlier versions of TanStack AI routed structured JSON deltas through a `TextPart` and required you to filter that part out of your renderer. That hack is gone — `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` on a structured-output run now routes into a dedicated `StructuredOutputPart` (with `raw`, `partial`, `data`, `status`, optional `errorMessage`). If your render loop still has an explicit `if (part.type === \"text\") return null;` line specifically for hiding structured JSON, you can remove it.\n\n> **Going lower-level?** `useChat` still exposes `onChunk` if you want to observe individual chunks alongside the managed `partial` / `final` state (e.g. to drive a custom progress UI). Internal partial/final tracking runs first, then your `onChunk` callback fires with the same chunk — the two paths compose.\n\n`useChat` (React, Vue, Solid) and `createChat` (Svelte) all accept the same `outputSchema` option and expose `partial` / `final` with the same semantics — only the reactivity primitive differs (React state, Vue `shallowRef`, Solid `Accessor`, Svelte reactive getter). See [Quick Start](../getting-started/quick-start) for the local idioms.\n\n## What the stream contains\n\n`chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` returns a `StructuredOutputStream<T>`. The stream includes a `CUSTOM` event named `structured-output.complete` before `RUN_FINISHED`. The completion event carries the parsed object. A parsing failure emits `RUN_ERROR` instead of a successful `RUN_FINISHED`.\n\n```typescript ignore\n{\n  type: \"CUSTOM\",\n  name: \"structured-output.complete\",\n  value: {\n    object: T;          // completed, parsed, typed\n    raw: string;        // full accumulated JSON text\n    reasoning?: string; // present only for thinking/reasoning models\n  },\n  // ...standard event fields (timestamp, …)\n  // model lives in metadata.tanstack when present\n}\n```\n\nA `structured-output.start` event fires once at the beginning of the run carrying `{ messageId }`. Its job is to tell the client \"the next batch of `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas belongs to the assistant message with this id — route them into a `StructuredOutputPart` instead of building a free-form `TextPart`.\" The runtime also attaches the same `messageId` to the terminal `structured-output.complete` event's `value` so the client snaps the right assistant message's part on the way out — that extra field isn't on the public `StructuredOutputCompleteEvent<T>` shape (since consumer code typically doesn't need it; the start event already carries it), but you can read it off `value` at runtime if you need to.\n\n## Adapter coverage\n\nStreaming structured output works with **every adapter**, but only some support a true single-request streaming wire format:\n\n| Adapter | Behavior with `outputSchema` + `stream: true` |\n|---|---|\n| `@tanstack/ai-openai` | Native single-request stream (Responses API, `text.format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-openrouter` | Native single-request stream (`response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-grok` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-groq` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-bedrock` | Native stream through Converse or an OpenAI-compatible API |\n| `@tanstack/ai-byteplus` | Native single-request stream on supported models; unsupported models emit `RUN_ERROR` |\n| `@tanstack/ai-llmgateway` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-cloudflare` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-lovable` | Native single-request stream (Responses or Chat Completions) |\n| Other adapters (anthropic, gemini, ollama, …) | Fallback: runs non-streaming `structuredOutput` and emits the final object as one `structured-output.complete` event |\n\nThe fallback path keeps the consumer code identical across providers — you always read the final object off `structured-output.complete` — but you won't see incremental deltas unless the adapter implements `structuredOutputStream` natively.\n\n## Advanced: iterating the stream directly\n\nWhen you don't need the SSE-over-HTTP boundary — Node scripts, CLIs, server endpoints that respond with a final JSON object instead of a stream, or tests — consume `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` as a plain async iterable:\n\n```typescript ignore\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John Doe is 30, john@example.com\" }],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  stream: true,\n});\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === \"CUSTOM\" && chunk.name === \"structured-output.complete\") {\n    // Typed against PersonSchema. Validate here when required.\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.name);\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.age);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is the same `StructuredOutputStream<T>` the server endpoint above hands to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. Pick this shape when you're a single process end-to-end; use the server-endpoint-plus-`useChat` shape when there's a network in the middle.\n\n> **Combining with tools?** When `outputSchema`, `stream: true`, and `tools` are all set, the agent loop runs first and the structured stream emits its terminal event only after every tool completes. Tool-approval gates and client-tool invocations work the same as in a normal chat — see [With Tools](./with-tools) for the full pause/resume pattern.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Streaming Structured Output UIs\nid: structured-outputs-streaming\norder: 3\ndescription: \"Build a UI that fills in field by field as the model streams structured JSON. chat({ outputSchema, stream: true }) on the server, useChat({ outputSchema }) on the client — progressive partial state plus a typed terminal object.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - structured outputs\n  - streaming\n  - useChat outputSchema\n  - partial\n  - final\n  - DeepPartial\n  - progressive ui\n---\n\nYou have an existing chat-style endpoint and you want the structured response to populate a UI _while_ the model is generating — a form filling in field by field, a card whose ingredients list grows as JSON streams in, a typewriter preview of a JSON-typed report. Blocking on `await chat({ outputSchema })` would leave the UI dark until the whole object is ready; this guide is the alternative.\n\nBy the end you'll have a server endpoint streaming structured JSON as Server-Sent Events, and a client that reads a typed `partial` (progressive object) and `final` (completed terminal object) from `useChat`.\n\nFor a full Start app that shows a table from `partial.rows`, open [Streaming Structured Table](../tutorials/streaming-structured-table).\n\n> **Note:** This is the streaming counterpart of [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot). If you don't need progressive UI updates, the one-shot path is simpler. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns and keep history, see [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn).\n\n## Server endpoint\n\n```typescript\n// app/api/extract-person/route.ts (or your framework's equivalent)\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nThat's the entire server side. `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` returns a `StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<typeof PersonSchema>>` — an `AsyncIterable` of standard streaming events plus a terminal `structured-output.complete` event carrying the completed object. `toServerSentEventsResponse` knows what to do with it.\n\n## Client with `useChat`\n\nPass the same schema to `useChat`. The hook gives you a progressively-parsed `partial` and a typed `final`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, partial, final } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <form\n      onSubmit={(e) => {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\");\n      }}\n    >\n      <button disabled={isLoading}>Extract</button>\n      {/* `partial` fills in field by field as JSON streams in. */}\n      <p>Name: {partial.name ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Age: {partial.age ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      <p>Email: {partial.email ?? \"…\"}</p>\n      {final && <pre>Completed: {JSON.stringify(final, null, 2)}</pre>}\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nWhat the hook does for you:\n\n- **`partial`** is `DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>` — every property optional, every nested array element optional. Updated from `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas via the runtime's partial-JSON parser. The hook derives it from the latest assistant message's `structured-output` part (see [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) for why that distinction matters), so it reads `{}` between `sendMessage()` and the first chunk without any extra reset state.\n- **`final`** is `z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null` — the completed terminal payload from the `structured-output.complete` event. `null` until the run completes successfully.\n- **`outputSchema`** is used purely for client-side TypeScript inference. The streaming path does not run Standard Schema validation; validate the completed object in the consumer when required.\n- The same shape works for **non-streaming adapters too**. If an adapter (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama) returns a single `structured-output.complete` event with no incremental deltas, `partial` stays `{}` and `final` populates when the event arrives. Same consumer code. Claude Code and Codex emit `structured-output.complete` from the harness event. OpenCode, Grok Build, and `acpCompatible` parse the last assistant text at the end. In both cases `partial` stays empty until `final` is set. See [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n`outputSchema` is optional: omit it and `useChat` returns its standard shape without `partial` / `final`.\n\n## Rendering reasoning and tool calls\n\n`partial` / `final` cover the structured payload. Reasoning tokens and tool calls land where they would in any other chat — on `messages[…].parts`:\n\n| Chunk type | Where it lands on `messages[i].parts` |\n|---|---|\n| `REASONING_MESSAGE_CONTENT` | `ThinkingPart` on the assistant message |\n| `TOOL_CALL_START` / `_ARGS` / `_END` | `ToolCallPart` on the assistant message |\n| `TOOL_CALL_RESULT` | `ToolResultPart` on the tool message |\n| `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` (with `outputSchema` set) | `StructuredOutputPart` on the assistant message — the JSON deltas accumulate into `part.raw` and the progressive parse populates `part.partial` |\n| `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` (no `outputSchema`) | `TextPart` on the assistant message |\n\nSo render reasoning, tool calls, and the typed object from `messages[].parts`. `final` and `partial` are shortcuts for the latest `structured-output` part only.\n\n```tsx ignore\nreturn (\n  <>\n    {messages.map((message) => (\n      <div key={message.id}>\n        {message.parts.map((part, i) => {\n          if (part.type === \"thinking\") {\n            return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n            return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"text\") {\n            return <p key={i}>{part.content}</p>;\n          }\n          if (part.type === \"structured-output\") {\n            return (\n              <StructuredView key={i} data={part.data ?? part.partial} />\n            );\n          }\n          return null;\n        })}\n      </div>\n    ))}\n  </>\n);\n```\n\nThe `structured-output` part fields:\n\n- `data`: the validated object after `structured-output.complete`\n- `partial`: a progressive parse of `raw` while JSON streams\n- `raw`: the source JSON string\n- `status`: `streaming`, `complete`, or `error`\n\n`final` equals `data` on the latest assistant turn. Walk `parts` when you need history, tool calls, or reasoning.\n\n> **Migration note:** Earlier versions of TanStack AI routed structured JSON deltas through a `TextPart` and required you to filter that part out of your renderer. That hack is gone — `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` on a structured-output run now routes into a dedicated `StructuredOutputPart` (with `raw`, `partial`, `data`, `status`, optional `errorMessage`). If your render loop still has an explicit `if (part.type === \"text\") return null;` line specifically for hiding structured JSON, you can remove it.\n\n> **Going lower-level?** `useChat` still exposes `onChunk` if you want to observe individual chunks alongside the managed `partial` / `final` state (e.g. to drive a custom progress UI). Internal partial/final tracking runs first, then your `onChunk` callback fires with the same chunk — the two paths compose.\n\n`useChat` (React, Vue, Solid) and `createChat` (Svelte) all accept the same `outputSchema` option and expose `partial` / `final` with the same semantics — only the reactivity primitive differs (React state, Vue `shallowRef`, Solid `Accessor`, Svelte reactive getter). See [Quick Start](../getting-started/quick-start) for the local idioms.\n\n## What the stream contains\n\n`chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` returns a `StructuredOutputStream<T>`. The stream includes a `CUSTOM` event named `structured-output.complete` before `RUN_FINISHED`. The completion event carries the parsed object. A parsing failure emits `RUN_ERROR` instead of a successful `RUN_FINISHED`.\n\n```typescript ignore\n{\n  type: \"CUSTOM\",\n  name: \"structured-output.complete\",\n  value: {\n    object: T;          // completed, parsed, typed\n    raw: string;        // full accumulated JSON text\n    reasoning?: string; // present only for thinking/reasoning models\n  },\n  // ...standard event fields (timestamp, …)\n  // model lives in metadata.tanstack when present\n}\n```\n\nA `structured-output.start` event fires once at the beginning of the run carrying `{ messageId }`. Its job is to tell the client \"the next batch of `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas belongs to the assistant message with this id — route them into a `StructuredOutputPart` instead of building a free-form `TextPart`.\" The runtime also attaches the same `messageId` to the terminal `structured-output.complete` event's `value` so the client snaps the right assistant message's part on the way out — that extra field isn't on the public `StructuredOutputCompleteEvent<T>` shape (since consumer code typically doesn't need it; the start event already carries it), but you can read it off `value` at runtime if you need to.\n\n## Adapter coverage\n\nStreaming structured output works with **every adapter**, but only some support a true single-request streaming wire format:\n\n| Adapter | Behavior with `outputSchema` + `stream: true` |\n|---|---|\n| `@tanstack/ai-openai` | Native single-request stream (Responses API, `text.format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-openrouter` | Native single-request stream (`response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-grok` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-groq` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-bedrock` | Native stream through Converse or an OpenAI-compatible API |\n| `@tanstack/ai-byteplus` | Native single-request stream on supported models; unsupported models emit `RUN_ERROR` |\n| `@tanstack/ai-llmgateway` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-cloudflare` | Native single-request stream (Chat Completions, `response_format: json_schema`) |\n| `@tanstack/ai-lovable` | Native single-request stream (Responses or Chat Completions) |\n| Other adapters (anthropic, gemini, ollama, …) | Fallback: runs non-streaming `structuredOutput` and emits the final object as one `structured-output.complete` event |\n\nThe fallback path keeps the consumer code identical across providers — you always read the final object off `structured-output.complete` — but you won't see incremental deltas unless the adapter implements `structuredOutputStream` natively.\n\n## Advanced: iterating the stream directly\n\nWhen you don't need the SSE-over-HTTP boundary — Node scripts, CLIs, server endpoints that respond with a final JSON object instead of a stream, or tests — consume `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` as a plain async iterable:\n\n```typescript ignore\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John Doe is 30, john@example.com\" }],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  stream: true,\n});\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === \"CUSTOM\" && chunk.name === \"structured-output.complete\") {\n    // Typed against PersonSchema. Validate here when required.\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.name);\n    console.log(chunk.value.object.age);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is the same `StructuredOutputStream<T>` the server endpoint above hands to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. Pick this shape when you're a single process end-to-end; use the server-endpoint-plus-`useChat` shape when there's a network in the middle.\n\n> **Combining with tools?** When `outputSchema`, `stream: true`, and `tools` are all set, the agent loop runs first and the structured stream emits its terminal event only after every tool completes. Tool-approval gates and client-tool invocations work the same as in a normal chat — see [With Tools](./with-tools) for the full pause/resume pattern.\n"}
